WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.326
A matrix can describe a linear transformation

00:00:03.326 --> 00:00:07.506
Its columns tell us where the standard basis vectors go

00:00:07.506 --> 00:00:15.867
This matrix leaves the horizontal basis vector fixed and sends the vertical basis vector to one, one, producing a shear.

00:00:17.167 --> 00:00:24.767
Any vector is a combination of the basis vectors, so its image is the same combination of their images

00:00:24.767 --> 00:00:30.767
One copy of the first column plus two copies of the second gives three, two

00:00:30.767 --> 00:00:33.167
Matrix multiplication encodes this geometric rule.

00:00:34.467 --> 00:00:38.719
A product of matrices composes transformations from right to left

00:00:38.719 --> 00:00:43.822
First B doubles the horizontal coordinate, sending one, one to two, one

00:00:43.822 --> 00:00:48.500
Then the shear A sends that intermediate vector to three, one.

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:52.997
Reversing the order gives a different result

00:00:52.997 --> 00:00:59.849
Shearing first sends one, one to two, one, and doubling horizontally afterward gives four, one

00:00:59.849 --> 00:01:06.700
Matrix products generally do not commute, and their dimensions must also be compatible before multiplication.
